# TCS NQT Practice Platform — Backend Project Init

> Generated analysis of the backend as of 2026-11-04.
> No source files were modified during this analysis.

---

## 1. Framework & Dependencies

**Stack:** Node.js + **Express 5.2** + **MongoDB via Mongoose 9**, deployed as a
**Vercel serverless function** (`vercel.json`, `@vercel/node`).

| Package | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `express` ^5.2.1 | Web framework (v5 — note: new query-parser behavior) |
| `mongoose` ^9.4.1 | MongoDB ODM |
| `jsonwebtoken` | Access + refresh JWT auth |
| `bcryptjs` | Password hashing (12 rounds) |
| `cors` | CORS middleware |
| `helmet` | Security headers |
| `cookie-parser` | Cookie parsing |
| `morgan` | HTTP request logging (dev only) |
| `express-validator` | Request validation |
| `express-rate-limit` | Rate limiting (only used on payment submit) |
| `razorpay` | Installed + `utils/razorpay.js` exists, but **never used** — payments are manual UPI/UTR verification |
| `nodemon` (dev) | Dev server with auto-reload |

**Scripts:** `start`, `dev`, plus dozens of `seed:*` commands (each inserts question
bank data). **No test framework** — zero automated tests.

---

## 2. Folder Structure

```
server.js               Entry point — DB connect, middleware, route mounting, debug endpoint
config/                 db.js (mongo connect), products.js (premium-lifetime product config)
models/                 User, Test, Question (+ PassageQuestion discriminator), TestAttempt,
                        Result, UserAnalytics, Payment, CodingQuestion
controllers/            authController, testController, attemptController, resultController,
                        analyticsController, adminController, codingController, paymentController
routes/                 Thin routers; admin routes use router.use(protect, adminOnly)
middleware/             auth.js (protect + adminOnly), premiumCheck.js (defined but unused),
                        validate.js (express-validator runner), errorHandler.js
validators/             auth / question / test express-validator chains
utils/                  generateToken, scoringEngine (result calculation),
                        recommendationEngine (analytics + recommendations), razorpay (dead code)
seeds/                  One-off seed scripts + JSON question banks (numerical/reasoning/verbal/...)
scripts/                DB maintenance utilities
```

### Patterns

- MVC-style layering (routes → controllers → models)
- Centralized error handler (handles Mongoose ValidationError / CastError / duplicate-key)
- Fisher–Yates shuffled random question selection per attempt
- Resume-able attempts (in-progress attempts can resume within duration + 30s grace)
- Section/topic scoring with time tracking
- `UserAnalytics` updated after each submission (running averages, score history last 50)
- Manual UPI → UTR → admin-approval payment flow for premium upgrades

---

## 3. Bugs & Security Issues

### 🔴 Critical

**1. Unauthenticated premium upgrade — `POST /api/auth/upgrade-premium`**
In `routes/auth.js` this route has **no `protect` middleware**, and
`authController.upgradePremium` accepts **any email in the request body** and sets
`isPremium = true`, `subscriptionType = 'pro'`, 1-year expiry. Anyone can grant
anyone (including themselves) premium for free with one request. This completely
bypasses the entire payment flow.

**2. Premium tests are accessible without paying — `startAttempt` lacks a premium check**
`GET /api/tests/:id` correctly blocks premium tests for non-premium users, but
`POST /api/attempts/start/:testId` (attemptController.js) never checks `test.isPremium`.
A free user can start any premium test (and seeded premium `topic_practice` tests) by
ID and receive the full question set. The `premiumOnly` middleware exists but is
**imported in routes/tests.js and never used anywhere**.

**3. Public debug endpoint leaks internals — `/api/debug/db`**
`server.js` exposes DB host, DB name, collection counts, and **all test titles/IDs**
with no auth. The source comment even says `REMOVE after debugging` — it is still present.

**4. Payment flow grants premium without expiry and without approval-state checks**
`approvePayment` sets `isPremium: true` but never sets `subscriptionType` or
`subscriptionExpiry` (unlike the admin upgrade path, which sets 1 year). It also
does not check whether the payment was already approved/rejected. Creates
inconsistent premium state and allows redundant mutation.

### 🟠 High

**5. Race condition → duplicate results & double-counted analytics**
`submitAttempt` does `findOne({ status: 'in_progress' })` → check → `save()` as
separate non-atomic steps. Two concurrent submits both pass the check, both create
`Result` documents and both increment analytics. Fix: atomic
`findOneAndUpdate({ status: 'in_progress' }, ...)`. `Result` also has no unique
index on `attempt`.

**6. No server-side time enforcement / unbounded client-supplied values**
- `saveAnswer` and `navigate` trust client-sent `timeSpent` and add it blindly —
  negative or huge values skew all time analytics.
- Answers are accepted even after the attempt's time limit expired; only submission
  marks it `timed_out`.
- No controller-level check that `selectedAnswer` is 0–3 (schema min/max catches it,
  but as a Mongoose validation error rather than a clean 400).

**7. Broken input validation → 500 crashes instead of 400s**
- `saveAnswer`: `questionIndex` isn't checked to be an integer (`"abc"` passes both
  comparisons; `responses["abc"]` is undefined → TypeError → 500).
- `navigate`: `previousQuestion < responses.length` has no lower bound —
  `previousQuestion = -1` → `responses[-1].timeSpent` throws → 500.
- `parseInt()` used everywhere without radix and without upper bound on `limit`
  pagination params (`?limit=10000000`).

**8. CORS misconfiguration**
`cors({ origin: true, credentials: true })` reflects **any** origin with credentials.
Additionally, `vercel.json` sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` together with
`Allow-Credentials: true` (an invalid/dangerous combo) for preflight requests.
Practical impact is limited because auth uses Bearer headers (not cookies), but it
defeats the purpose of CORS restrictions — should be an explicit allowlist of
frontend origins.

**9. No rate limiting on auth endpoints**
Only `/api/payment/submit` is limited (5/hour). `/api/auth/login` and
`/api/auth/register` are unlimited → credential brute-force, user enumeration,
account spam. No lockout either.

### 🟡 Medium

**10. N+1 query in analytics — `getSectionAnalysis`**
Inside a loop over every topic in `topicPerformance`, it runs
`Result.find({ user })` **each iteration** — O(topics × results) DB round-trips.
Fetch once, or use an aggregation pipeline.

**11. Admin dashboard loads the entire results collection**
`Result.find().select('percentage')` pulls **every result ever recorded** into memory
to average them. Should be a Mongo `aggregate($group avg)`.

**12. Mass assignment (admin-only, lower risk)**
`addQuestion` / `updateQuestion` / `updateTest` pass raw `req.body` straight to
Mongoose. Strict schema drops unknown fields, but explicit field whitelisting is safer.

**13. `generatePracticeTest` creates a new `Test` document per request**
For topics without a seeded test, every call inserts a new `topic_practice` Test into
the DB — unbounded collection growth from ordinary user traffic. These dynamic tests
are also never cleaned up.

**14. Coding questions (with full solutions) are fully public**
`/api/coding` routes have no `protect` at all — anyone can scrape all problems +
brute-force/optimal solutions. Given the paid-premium model, this likely contradicts
the business intent (verify against frontend expectations).

**15. Error handler leaks internal messages in production**
For non-Mongoose errors it always returns `err.message` (which may contain DB/driver
internals) with HTTP 500. Only stack traces are dev-gated. Unknown 500s should return
a generic message in production.

**16. Refresh tokens stored in plaintext in DB; access tokens not revocable**
Refresh tokens are compared directly against `user.refreshToken`. Hashing them would
limit blast radius if the DB leaks. Acceptable trade-off in many apps, but worth flagging.

**17. `express.json({ limit: '10mb' })`**
Far larger than this API needs (question payloads are small); increases DoS surface.
Reduce to ~100kb–1mb.

### 🟢 Low / hygiene

- `utils/razorpay.js` and the `razorpay` dependency are **dead code** (payment is
  manual UTR) — remove or wire up properly. Same for `GROK_KEY` in `.env` if unused.
- No JSON **404 handler** — unknown routes fall through to Express's default HTML 404.
- `console.log` of premium test titles/IDs in `getTests` — noisy, leaks to logs.
- Passwords minimum length 6 — weak; consider 8+.
- `totalTime: undefined` leftover key in `scoringEngine.sectionWise` (silently dropped
  by the strict schema, but sloppy).
- Serverless: `connectDB()` runs per cold start with no cached connection across
  invocations; `app.listen()` at module scope. Works on Vercel, but caching the
  Mongoose connection globally is the recommended pattern to avoid connection storms.
- Missing DB indexes: `Payment.user` / `Payment.status` (pending-list query path).
- **Secrets hygiene (currently OK, but watch it):** `.env` and `rzp-key.csv` are
  correctly gitignored and **not** in git history. They still sit in the working tree
  alongside tracked files. If `rzp-key.csv` contains **live** Razorpay keys, rotate
  them — never commit. Also `preeemi,json` (note comma — likely an accidental
  filename) and large PDFs are repo clutter.
- **No tests at all** — no Jest/Mocha; high regression risk for an auth + payments +
  scoring system.
- **Uncommitted working-tree changes** — modified files (`config/products.js`,
  `controllers/*`, `routes/*`, `models/*`, `seeds/*.json`) and untracked files
  (`PrimiumQuesiton.txt`, `RAZORPAY_FRONTEND.md`, `preeemi,json`, `seeds/premium_*.json`, …)
  appear to be in-progress work; treat them as such.
- `.aider.chat.history.md` — committed AI-chat logs can contain pasted secrets/prompts;
  review before pushing.
- Inconsistent docs: `API_PAYMENT.md` documents sending `amount` in the request body,
  but the server correctly derives amount from `productId` — docs are stale/misleading.
- `CLAUDE.md` says 15m access tokens; utils actually uses the `JWT_EXPIRE` env var.

---

## 4. Priority Fix List (recommended order)

1. Delete or protect `/api/auth/upgrade-premium` (route does nothing legitimate).
2. Add premium check to `startAttempt` (or apply `premiumOnly` consistently).
3. Remove `/api/debug/db`.
4. Make attempt submission atomic (`findOneAndUpdate` with `status: 'in_progress'`
   filter) + unique index on `Result.attempt`.
5. Validate `questionIndex` / `previousQuestion` / `timeSpent`; enforce the time limit
   server-side.
6. Fix CORS to an allowlist; add rate limiting to `/api/auth/*`.
7. Fix N+1 in `getSectionAnalysis`; aggregate for admin avg score.
8. Remove dead Razorpay code or complete the integration; tighten JSON body limit.

---

## 5. API Surface Summary

| Prefix | Auth | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `/api/auth` | Mixed (register/login/refresh/upgrade-premium are PUBLIC) | Register, login, refresh, logout, me |
| `/api/tests` | protect | List tests, get test, topics, practice questions, generate practice test |
| `/api/attempts` | protect | Start, answer, navigate, mark review, tab-switch, submit |
| `/api/results` | protect | History, detailed result, full question review |
| `/api/analytics` | protect | Dashboard, section/topic performance, trends, recommendations |
| `/api/admin` | protect + adminOnly | CRUD questions/tests, user management, dashboard stats |
| `/api/coding` | **NONE (public)** | PYQ coding question solutions with brute force & optimal approaches |
| `/api/payment` | protect (admin routes + adminOnly) | Manual UPI/UTR submission, admin approval → premium |
| `/api/health` | public | Health check |
| `/api/debug/db` | **PUBLIC — REMOVE** | Dumps DB host/name/counts/test list |

